data: {"type":"agent_chunk","agent":"researcher","content":"- CRISPR gene editing is a genetic engineering technique that modifies genomes and is based on a simplified version of the bacterial CRISPR-Cas9 antiviral defense system [1].\n- Cas9 nuclease, complexed with a synthetic guide RNA, is delivered into a cell, which allows the genome to be cut at a desired location [1].\n- Genome editing inserts, deletes, modifies, or replaces DNA at site-specific locations, using programmable molecules that create double-strand breaks [3].\n- In prokaryotes, CRISPR sequences derive from bacteriophage DNA and are used to detect and destroy similar phages during later infections [2].\n- Linked headlines report CRISPR work aimed at treating a broad array of diseases [4] and eliminating HIV in cell culture [5]."}

